.TH UPTIME 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uptime \- show how long the system has been running
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uptime
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uptime
prints a single line containing the current local time, how long the
system has been up, the number of logged-in users, and the load averages
over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes:
.PP
.EX
 12:34:56 up 1 day,  2:03,  1 user,  load average: 0.00, 0.00, 0.00
.EE
.PP
Uptimes of less than an hour are shown as
.IB N " min" ;
from one hour on as
.IR hh : mm ,
preceded by a day count once the system has been up a day or more.
.PP
The uptime is read from
.I /proc/uptime
and the load averages from
.IR /proc/loadavg ;
if either cannot be read, zero is shown.
The user count is the number of
.B USER_PROCESS
records with a non-empty user name in the utmp file; it is 0 if that
file cannot be opened.
.PP
.B uptime
takes no options; any arguments are ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B uptime
always exits with status 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/uptime
Seconds since boot.
.TP
.I /proc/loadavg
Load averages.
.TP
.I /var/run/utmp
Current login records.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR w (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR sysinfo (2),
.BR proc (5),
.BR utmp (5)
